\section{Methodology}{


For Pearson's \code{r} The package uses Fisher's variance-stabilising transform
\deqn{z = \mathrm{atanh}(r) = \tfrac{1}{2}\ln\!\frac{1+r}{1-r},}
which is approximately Gaussian with variance \eqn{1/(n-3)} under the
null of no correlation (Fisher, 1915; 1921). The confidence interval on
\code{z} is
\deqn{z \pm t_{1-\alpha/2,\,n-3}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{n-3}},}
which is then back-transformed to the correlation scale via
\eqn{r = \mathrm{tanh}(z)}. The package uses a Student-t critical value rather than
the Gaussian one; this gives a slightly wider interval for small \code{n} and
is what \code{stats::cor.test} uses internally for Pearson intervals.

For Spearman's \eqn{\rho}, the same Fisher-z approximation is applied to
the point estimate. This works well for moderate \code{n} (Bonett & Wright,
2000), although for very small samples or extreme correlations the
coverage can be slightly off.

For Kendall's \eqn{\tau}, \code{stats::cor.test} is called with
\code{exact = FALSE} to avoid the combinatorial cost of the exact variance
computation when \code{n} is large. The asymptotic variance used is the one
for Kendall rank association. Confidence intervals for Kendall's tau are also
constructed via the Fisher-z approximation on \code{atanh(tau)}.

When a \code{group} is supplied, p-values are adjusted across groups using
\code{stats::p.adjust} with the requested method.
}

\section{Limitations}{

\itemize{
\item The Fisher-z interval for Spearman and Kendall is approximate and can
be inaccurate for very small samples or when the correlation is close
to \eqn{\pm 1}. For \code{n < 10} consider exact permutation or bootstrap
intervals instead.
\item Kendall's tau with ties uses the normal approximation built into
\code{cor.test}; the exact variance is not computed.
\item Groupwise correlations discard groups with fewer than 3 finite
observations. The reported adjusted p-values correspond only to the
groups that were actually computed; do not interpret them as a global
omnibus test.
\item Pooling across groups via a single correlation ignores possible
between-group heterogeneity. If the analyst supplies a \code{group}, read the
\code{groupwise} table; the top-level \code{correlation} is the pooled estimate
and may be misleading if the groups differ in location or scale.
}
